{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 40,
      "why": "One result/cycle makes the 32-request burst trivially meet sys_p99_batch_us (needs only ~(N+33)/Fmax <= 0.5us, i.e. Fmax >= ~90-110 MHz, easy for a single micro-rotation stage in sky130). It is the reference area/accuracy trade-off: area grows with N register stages, so the interesting question is how small W and N can go while still reaching 12 accuracy bits."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 6, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 40,
      "why": "Same 1 result/cycle throughput but registers only every m stages, cutting pipeline FFs (the dominant area term) at the cost of Fmax. m=2..4 should still clear the burst bound while saving area; m>=6 likely drops below the ~64 results/us the 0.5us batch needs, so the front should show exactly where that cliff is."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -2, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 3, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 20,
      "why": "Middle ground: k micro-rotations/cycle with a shared datapath gives k/(ceil(N/k)+3) results/cycle, so large k can still reach the burst rate with fewer register banks than a fully pipelined chain. Worth a smaller share to check whether its shared-datapath area beats pipelined_m at equal accuracy."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The binding system constraint is the burst: 32 requests must land within 0.5us of the tick, i.e. ~64 results/us sustained, which is far above the nominal 32 results/us. That rules out `iterative` (throughput = Fmax/(N+3), and 12-bit accuracy forces N>=~13, giving at best ~25 results/us even at an implausible 400 MHz), so I spend nothing on it. Accuracy depends only on (W, N, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding): the residual-angle term needs N>=~13 and the accumulated shift/quantisation term needs W+frac_guard>=~17, so I centre the boxes on W 14..22, N 12..20, frac_guard 0..3, angle_guard -2..3 and let the optimiser find the cheapest corner that still clears 2^-12. The three pipelined/unrolled families all deliver >=1 result/cycle and are the only ones that can plausibly satisfy the batch bound; the real trade-off to map is registers (area) versus Fmax, which is exactly what m and k control. Round 1 is deliberately broad on W/N/guards and narrow on m (2..6) and k (2..8), since the burst bound will cut the large-m/large-k end."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Budget exhausted (400/400 evals, round 4 of 4) and the design space is feasible, so `stop` (not `infeasible`). Evidence: 225 of 400 evaluations are feasible (177 unique) and the front holds 28 designs, so every spec constraint is reachable — throughput best seen 163 MSPS vs >=32 required, best max_abs_err 2^-24.26 vs <=2^-12 required, best sys_p99_batch_us 0.282 vs <=0.5 required. The min-area selection rule is also satisfied: the front's cheapest design (pipelined data_width=16 n_iter=14 angle_guard=3 frac_guard=4 rounding=round, area_um2=42399, throughput 132 MSPS) still clears the accuracy floor at 12.5 bits > 12 (max_abs_err 1.7e-4 <= 2.44e-4). The front spans area_um2 42399..83872 against HV ref 100000 and accuracy_bits 12.5..19.1 against ref 12, giving HV 2.812e+05 with only +0.9% gain this round, i.e. converged. The unexplored `iterative` family and the 0-feasible `unrolled_k`/`pipelined_m` families cannot improve on this: unrolled_k peaked at 5.13 MSPS throughput (constraint is 32) and neither reached a feasible design across 60 combined evaluations. Hypervolume gain is marginal and no budget remains for mapping the front further, so the front is good enough to stop."
}
